1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,433
Look inside this motor.

2
00:00:02,483 --> 00:00:06,412
The copper windings stay still, while the
magnets turn with the shaft.

3
00:00:06,452 --> 00:00:08,438
There's a small air gap between them —

4
00:00:08,478 --> 00:00:11,498
nothing is pushing through a mechanical
contact.

5
00:00:11,538 --> 00:00:13,921
So where does the turning force come from?

6
00:00:14,221 --> 00:00:19,348
To answer that, let's start with a much
simpler experiment: a wire and a compass.

7
00:00:20,648 --> 00:00:25,470
In the spring of 1820, during a lecture in
Copenhagen, Hans Christian Ørsted

8
00:00:25,510 --> 00:00:30,619
placed a wire carrying current just above
a compass needle, parallel to it.

9
00:00:30,659 --> 00:00:32,374
The needle swung aside.

10
00:00:32,414 --> 00:00:35,852
After careful tests, he published the
result that July:

11
00:00:35,892 --> 00:00:39,364
an electric current produces a magnetic
effect.

12
00:00:40,564 --> 00:00:42,398
Let's rebuild that experiment.

13
00:00:42,438 --> 00:00:45,527
With the current off, the needle follows
the horizontal

14
00:00:45,567 --> 00:00:49,082
part of Earth's field — about twenty
microtesla here.

15
00:00:49,182 --> 00:00:51,789
Switch the current on, and it turns west.

16
00:00:52,589 --> 00:00:54,836
Reverse it, and it turns east.

17
00:00:55,536 --> 00:00:59,929
The needle doesn't respond to the wire
alone. It lines up with the total

18
00:00:59,969 --> 00:01:04,594
local field: Earth's field plus the wire's
field, added as vectors.

19
00:01:05,794 --> 00:01:08,315
So what does the wire's own field look
like?

20
00:01:08,355 --> 00:01:10,253
Calculated in three dimensions,

21
00:01:10,293 --> 00:01:14,655
it forms circles around the wire, in
planes perpendicular to it.

22
00:01:14,695 --> 00:01:17,565
Point your right thumb along the
conventional current,

23
00:01:17,605 --> 00:01:20,497
and your fingers curl the way the field
points.

24
00:01:20,537 --> 00:01:25,068
The arrowheads only mark direction.
Nothing flows along these lines.

25
00:01:26,268 --> 00:01:27,551
How strong is it?

26
00:01:27,591 --> 00:01:31,507
What we now call Ampère's law says: go
around any closed path,

27
00:01:31,547 --> 00:01:35,863
add up B·dl, and you get μ₀ times the
current passing through.

28
00:01:35,903 --> 00:01:38,797
For a long, straight wire, symmetry does
the work.

29
00:01:38,837 --> 00:01:41,674
On a circle of radius r, centred on the
wire,

30
00:01:41,714 --> 00:01:45,413
B runs along the path, with the same size
everywhere.

31
00:01:45,453 --> 00:01:51,492
So the integral is just B times 2πr, and B
of r is μ₀I over 2πr.

32
00:01:51,532 --> 00:01:56,218
That assumes a long, thin wire, a steady
current, and no iron nearby.

33
00:01:57,418 --> 00:01:59,482
Let's predict something. Two amps,

34
00:01:59,522 --> 00:02:03,533
one centimetre from the wire, gives about
forty microtesla.

35
00:02:03,573 --> 00:02:05,612
That's twice Earth's horizontal field,

36
00:02:05,652 --> 00:02:09,021
so the needle should turn by about sixty-
three degrees.

37
00:02:09,061 --> 00:02:11,373
Double the distance, and the field halves.

38
00:02:11,413 --> 00:02:15,357
Now the two fields are equal, and the
needle sits at forty-five.

39
00:02:15,397 --> 00:02:19,244
A bench supply, a ruler and a compass are
enough to check it.

40
00:02:20,444 --> 00:02:23,705
To concentrate the field, bend the wire
into a loop.

41
00:02:23,745 --> 00:02:27,826
At a point on the axis, each short piece
of wire adds a small field,

42
00:02:27,866 --> 00:02:30,711
at right angles to the line joining them.

43
00:02:30,751 --> 00:02:35,455
Pieces on opposite sides tilt in mirror
image: their sideways parts cancel,

44
00:02:35,495 --> 00:02:37,843
and their parts along the axis add.

45
00:02:39,043 --> 00:02:41,607
The Biot–Savart law makes this exact.

46
00:02:41,647 --> 00:02:45,899
Each current element, I times dl,
contributes μ₀ over 4π,

47
00:02:45,939 --> 00:02:49,235
times I dl cross r-hat, over r squared.

48
00:02:49,275 --> 00:02:55,205
Summed around a coil of N turns and radius
a, the axial field at distance x

49
00:02:55,245 --> 00:03:01,624
from the centre is μ₀NIa², over two, times
a² plus x², to the three-halves.

50
00:03:01,664 --> 00:03:06,639
That holds for a thin coil, with steady
current, in air — and only on the axis.

51
00:03:07,841 --> 00:03:10,582
Off the axis, we compute the field
numerically,

52
00:03:10,622 --> 00:03:13,822
summing seven hundred and twenty short
segments.

53
00:03:13,862 --> 00:03:17,980
Every line threads the loop, wraps around
the outside, and returns.

54
00:03:18,020 --> 00:03:20,707
From a distance, it looks just like a bar
magnet,

55
00:03:20,747 --> 00:03:23,195
with a north face and a south face.

56
00:03:24,395 --> 00:03:27,715
So we can describe the loop by its
magnetic moment, m:

57
00:03:27,755 --> 00:03:31,724
turns, times current, times area, pointing
along the axis.

58
00:03:31,764 --> 00:03:34,713
In a uniform field there's no net force,

59
00:03:34,753 --> 00:03:39,074
but there is a torque: τ = m × B, with
size mB sin θ.

60
00:03:39,114 --> 00:03:43,292
It's largest at ninety degrees, and zero
when m lines up with B.

61
00:03:43,332 --> 00:03:46,516
Let go, and the loop swings into line, and
stops.

62
00:03:46,556 --> 00:03:50,141
To keep it turning, the field has to keep
moving ahead of it.

63
00:03:51,341 --> 00:03:54,554
Faraday got continuous motion a different
way,

64
00:03:54,594 --> 00:03:58,722
in September 1821, at the Royal
Institution in London.

65
00:03:58,762 --> 00:04:01,466
A magnet stood upright in a basin of
mercury.

66
00:04:01,506 --> 00:04:05,613
A free wire dipped into the mercury beside
it. With current flowing,

67
00:04:05,653 --> 00:04:09,367
the wire circled the magnet's pole — and
kept circling.

68
00:04:09,407 --> 00:04:13,638
It was a demonstration, not a practical
motor. But it was the first

69
00:04:13,678 --> 00:04:18,386
continuous conversion of electrical energy
into mechanical motion.

70
00:04:19,586 --> 00:04:23,065
A modern motor moves the field
electrically instead.

71
00:04:23,105 --> 00:04:26,548
Three fixed windings, a hundred and twenty
degrees apart,

72
00:04:26,588 --> 00:04:30,736
carry sinusoidal currents, each delayed by
a third of a cycle.

73
00:04:30,776 --> 00:04:35,089
Each winding alone just pulses back and
forth along its own axis.

74
00:04:35,129 --> 00:04:38,010
But added as vectors, their fundamental
component

75
00:04:38,050 --> 00:04:41,152
keeps a constant length, and rotates
steadily.

76
00:04:41,192 --> 00:04:46,059
The rotor magnets follow at exactly that
speed. That's what synchronous means.

77
00:04:46,099 --> 00:04:48,504
Under load, the rotor lags by an angle,

78
00:04:48,544 --> 00:04:53,056
and the torque follows the sine of that
angle — just like m × B.

79
00:04:54,356 --> 00:04:56,635
Three predictions before next time.

80
00:04:56,675 --> 00:05:01,763
One: a long wire carries six amps. What's
the field three centimetres away?

81
00:05:03,063 --> 00:05:08,480
Two: how far along the axis of a coil does
the field fall to half its centre value?

82
00:05:09,780 --> 00:05:14,319
Three: swap two of the three phase leads.
What does the rotating field do?

83
00:05:15,619 --> 00:05:18,623
Next time, we'll see how real windings sit
in slots,

84
00:05:18,663 --> 00:05:22,089
and how their geometry shapes that
rotating field.
